From 15187cf9df341cfd6dcbdeaf40616b8562ddd814 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ren=C3=A9=20Rebe?= <rene@exactco.de>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 15:18:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] platform/x86: transmeta-ptt: Add Crusoe Persistent
 Translation Technology
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The Code Morphing Software (CMS) of Transmeta Crusoe processors can
export its x86 translations as a database and import them again, so
code does not have to be translated anew after a reboot. CMS identifies
a guest code page by the SHA-1 of its contents and installs the saved
translations of a page on its first translation miss. The database is
bound to the processor by a PTT key in the boot ROM and protected by a
keyed SHA-1; CMS rejects modified or foreign databases. CMS holds one
database at a time: a load replaces the previous one, a save exports
the translations live at that moment, and recording for a save starts
with the end of a load.

Transmeta shipped this for Windows (CrusoePTT 2.0.13); this driver
implements the same CMS protocol (PTT 2.0, CMS 4.4.0) as mechanisms
only, user space decides when to use them:

 - /dev/transmeta-ptt: opened read-only it streams a save, opened
   write-only it loads a database (empty: start recording), close()
   reports a rejected one;
 - sysfs: state, version, stats (the CMS PTT counters), last_load,
   abort, filter_pid (CR3 filter: export only the translations of one
   address space);
 - keygen, which makes CMS program a new key into the boot ROM, only
   exists with allow_keygen=1 and needs a magic value; the driver never
   generates a key by itself, without a key it only reports "nokey";
 - start_recording=1 ends an empty load at probe if the boot loader
   loaded nothing; off by default.

The system database is meant to be loaded by the boot loader before the
kernel (a separate GRUB module) and saved by init scripts.

The protocol was reverse engineered from the Windows driver and CMS
4.4.0 and tested in a CMS simulator; it has not run on real hardware.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: René Rebe <rene@exactco.de>
