@@ -0,0 +1,96 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+
+==================================================
+Transmeta Crusoe Persistent Translation Technology
+==================================================
+
+The Code Morphing Software (CMS) of Transmeta Crusoe processors translates
+x86 code into native VLIW code at run time. Persistent Translation
+Technology (PTT) lets CMS export these translations as a database and import
+them again, so that code which ran before a reboot does not have to be
+translated anew. The ``transmeta-ptt`` driver (``CONFIG_TRANSMETA_PTT``)
+gives user space access to it; CMS 4.4.0 implements PTT 2.0.
+
+How CMS uses a database
+=======================
+
+CMS identifies a guest code page by the SHA-1 of its 4 KiB contents. On the
+first translation miss in a page whose hash is in the loaded database, CMS
+installs all saved translations of that page. Translations therefore match
+by page contents, independent of the process, the virtual address or the
+file the code came from; a changed page simply does not match. Saved
+translations do not contain chained exits; CMS links them again on use.
+
+CMS holds one database: a load replaces the previous one. A save exports
+the translations CMS holds at that moment (including installed ones), not
+the parts of a loaded database that were never used, and CMS only records
+translations for a save after a load has ended, which may be an empty one.
+
+The database is bound to the processor: CMS only enables PTT when the boot
+ROM holds a PTT key that matches the processor identification, and it
+protects the database with a SHA-1 keyed with it. CMS rejects a database
+that was modified or saved on another processor. Without a key the state is
+``nokey`` and the driver does nothing but report it.
+
+Interface
+=========
+
+The driver provides mechanisms only; it does not load or save by itself.
+
+``/dev/transmeta-ptt`` (mode 0600) serves one session at a time and streams
+the database in chunks of up to 64 KiB per CMS call:
+
+- Opened read-only, it streams a save of the current translations; the end
+  of the data is end of file. CMS may take a while before the first byte.
+  Closing before the end aborts the save.
+- Opened write-only, it loads a database. CMS checks the database when the
+  file is closed; ``close()`` then returns ``-EBADMSG`` for a rejected
+  database, and ``last_load`` shows the result of the last load. Opening and
+  closing without data is an empty load, which starts the recording of new
+  translations without a database.
+
+The attributes in ``/sys/class/misc/transmeta-ptt/`` are documented in
+``Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-driver-transmeta-ptt``:
+
+``state``, ``version``, ``stats``, ``last_load``
+	CMS PTT state (``idle``, ``load``, ``save``, ``error``, ``nokey``,
+	...), the PTT version, the CMS counters and the last load result.
+``abort``
+	Writing 1 abandons a transfer CMS still holds.
+``filter_pid``
+	Limit what CMS marks for a save to the translations made while the
+	address space of this process runs (0 = all).
+``keygen``
+	Only present with the module parameter ``allow_keygen=1``. Writing
+	``program-boot-rom`` makes CMS generate a new key and **program it
+	into the boot ROM**. The driver never does this on its own, also not
+	in state ``nokey``. A failed ROM write can leave the system
+	unbootable; databases saved with an older key are no longer
+	accepted.
+
+Module parameters: ``start_recording=1`` ends an empty load at probe when
+nothing was loaded, so a save later in the boot has data; ``allow_keygen``
+as above. Both default to off.
+
+Use
+===
+
+When to load and save is a policy of the init scripts. The intended setup is
+one system database:
+
+- The boot loader loads it before the kernel starts (GRUB module
+  ``transmeta_ptt``: ``insmod transmeta_ptt`` and ``transmeta_ptt_load
+  /boot/transmeta-ptt.db`` before ``linux``), so the kernel and early user
+  space run from it. A later load from Linux would replace it.
+- Without a boot loader load, an empty load starts the recording::
+
+	: > /dev/transmeta-ptt
+
+- A save exports what is live: early in the boot it still holds the boot
+  path, at shutdown the working set of the session::
+
+	cat /dev/transmeta-ptt > /boot/transmeta-ptt.db.new &&
+		mv /boot/transmeta-ptt.db.new /boot/transmeta-ptt.db
+
+Per-program databases, as the Windows driver loads them on process start,
+evict the boot database at each load and are not recommended.
